## Recommended flow

```text
User opens cat-reply deep link
        ↓
handler validates token
        ↓
FSM stores internal parent/thread ID
        ↓
user sends cat video
        ↓
handler extracts Telegram input only
        ↓
CatReplyService.submit(...)
        ↓
repository creates submission
        ↓
[optional moderator approval]
        ↓
Telegram publisher sends video
    replying to parent's published MessageRef
        ↓
repository records returned MessageRef
        ↓
user receives confirmation
```

If moderation is required, insert it explicitly:

```text
submission
   ↓
moderation chat
   ↓
Approve / Reject callback
   ↓
service decision
   ↓
publish
```

Persist the moderator message as another `TelegramMessageRef`; don't make a secondary channel part of the thread identity.

---

# Callback/deep-link design

I would also abandon payloads such as:

```text
answer:<user_id>:<telegram_message_id>
```

for new code.

Prefer:

```text
catreply:<internal-id>
catmod:approve:<submission-id>
catmod:reject:<submission-id>
```

or aiogram 3's typed `CallbackData`.

For a public channel button that must open a private bot conversation, use **only a URL/deep link**:

```text
/start cat_<opaque-token>
```

Then resolve that token to your internal post/thread.

That prevents Telegram transport IDs from becoming your database/API contract.

---

# What to preserve vs. reject

| Existing Wall Idea | Cat feature |
|---|---|
| Deep-link from channel into private bot | **Preserve** |
| Telegram-native channel replies | **Preserve** |
| Record returned Telegram message ID | **Preserve, but pair with chat ID** |
| FSM for collecting next user message | **Preserve conceptually** |
| Content-specific giant handler set | **Collapse/thin out** |
| Model + engine + handlers in one file | **Reject** |
| Hard-coded bot/channel usernames | **Reject** |
| Raw user/message IDs in callback protocol | **Replace with internal IDs/tokens** |
| Broad `except Exception` everywhere | **Replace with typed failures + final safety boundary** |
| synchronous ORM directly in async handler | **Reject** |
| `@AfsarieCh` forwarding/sandbox behavior | **Do not reuse** |
| fire-and-forget background publishing | **Do not introduce** |

### Bottom line

The most valuable thing to steal from «دیوار ناشناس» is **the topology**, not the code:

```text
public Telegram message
        ↓ deep link
private submission
        ↓
resolve original author/thread
        ↓
publish/deliver reply
        ↓
retain Telegram message coordinates
```

For the cat-video feature, I would make **`thread_id`/`parent_id` the truth and `(chat_id, message_id)` the Telegram projection**. That one distinction removes most of the fragility currently accumulated in `post.py` and `message.py`, while still producing the same natural Telegram reply-chain experience.